\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Large language models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable
performance across diverse tasks, yet their architectures
remain fundamentally static. Once training completes, an
LLM's parameters are frozen: it cannot grow new capacity,
cannot correct specific knowledge without retraining,
and cannot forget selectively. This rigidity creates three
well-documented failures:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Failure 1 (Fixed Capacity).} A model deployed
    on a new domain either overfits (small model) or wastes
    compute (large model). No mechanism exists to grow capacity
    only when needed.
    \item \textbf{Failure 2 (Catastrophic Forgetting).}
    Fine-tuning on a new task destroys performance on previous
    tasks. Existing solutions (EWC, PackNet, LoRA) reduce
    forgetting but cannot eliminate it.
    \item \textbf{Failure 3 (No Localized Correction).}
    Correcting a single fact requires retraining millions of
    parameters. There is no surgical mechanism to update one
    piece of knowledge without perturbing the rest.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{The Gap}

Three research communities have attacked these failures
separately.

\textbf{Mixture-of-Experts (MoE)} addresses capacity by
activating a subset of experts per input. But experts are
fixed at initialization: no new experts can be added without
retraining the router. MoE also does not address forgetting
or localized correction.

\textbf{Continual Learning} addresses forgetting through
regularization (EWC) or parameter isolation (PackNet,
Progressive Nets). But these methods apply to small models
and do not scale to billion-parameter LLMs. They also do not
provide growth.

\textbf{Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)} addresses
localized knowledge by retrieving from an external store. But
RAG does not modify model weights: it cannot learn new
capabilities, only surface existing text.

No existing work unifies growth, federation, and
zero-forgetting under a single theoretical framework.

\subsection{Our Contribution}

We present Berna R5, an architecture that addresses all three
failures simultaneously. Our contributions are:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Architecture.} A model in which knowledge is
    stored in dynamic weighted cells. Cells are born when the
    model encounters new domains, split when saturated, and
    interconnect via a co-activation plexus. A
    100-chromosome DNA Kernel regulates cell behavior.

    \item \textbf{Theory.} We present two mathematical theorems
    (Balanced Growth Equilibrium and Plexus Connectivity
    Convergence), two design invariants (Transactional
    Incorporation and Protected Retention), and two empirical
    hypotheses (Saturation-Triggered Splitting and Bounded
    Forgetting).

    \item \textbf{Empirical Validation.} Six experiments on a
    50M-parameter PoC trained on 800M tokens.

    \item \textbf{Open Source.} Complete implementation including
    SQL registry, hardware-adaptive deployment, and atomic
    checkpointing for power-loss recovery.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{What We Do Not Claim}

We make no claim of SOTA on any benchmark. Our model is 50M
parameters, far smaller than production LLMs. We compare
against 50M baselines, not GPT-4-class systems. We make no
claim of theoretical completeness: all these results rest on
explicitly stated assumptions. Counterexamples are documented
in each proof.

